 {:seon.task/name "runtime-for-reads-the-pins-from-the-branch", :seon.task/plan "DESIGN, approved by root 2026-11-14 (estimate first, owner's rule): two identities. (1) BINARY: a digest of exactly what only a build can change, the build's own inputs. They are stated once, as the data bin/build runs from (resources/seon/config/build.edn: each step and the paths and pins it reads), so nothing restates them: seon.system/build-input? (a hand list: deps.edn, bin/build, .gitmodules, native/, resources/seon/config/, three env classes) is derived from that file and deleted. One implementation computes the digest (git blob ids of those paths plus the pin entities), called by bin/build (written beside the binary as target/seon.digest, replacing target/seon.tree as an identity) and by seon.fx/runtime-for at the one start. A mismatch builds once. (2) PROGRAM: the branch's forms as datoms; at the one start runtime's load-diff compares the program the binary recorded (target/seon.program) with the branch's and loads exactly the differing forms, O(d). Estimate: (1) one digest of ~20 paths and 16 pins at start, ~ms, no build unless a build input moved; (2) d form loads (2.46M ticks each today; eval-by-digest in the Jolt pin cuts that). Rejected: forms in the binary digest (every src edit would rebuild target/seon, ~93 s + ~40 s warm, measured 2026-11-14). CORE (root, from the owner: \"this shouldn't be possible\", a run on a binary its branch no longer matches; moved up to right after the fork bound, ahead of the Datahike pin): a binary's identity is the digest of exactly what it is built from (the branch's forms, its pins, deps.edn). It is current iff that digest equals its branch's, checked once at the one start; a mismatch builds. Evidence [M]: three checks disagreed on data's land: the plan said \"current by reach\" and built nothing, then bin/check refused the same binary. A second instance [M, data from the store]: on agent lb76's branch :main/task-seon-repl-an-uncited-public-defn-is-told-at-its-eval, tx 536878038 (2026-11-13T14:05:44Z), an eval under runtime fingerprint dev-DE9D22-9D4CA8D1-2114B5.CCCC55EA.111D38.744A1C9B-tr retracted only :var/form of seon.system/baked-paths, which main's import had deleted whole three minutes earlier (tx 536877923, a fingerprint without -tr): the branch recorded main's deletion as its own unmap, and its dead form turned seon.main-test/every-name-a-form-uses-is-a-var red there (reproduce: seon.system/branch-db and a history read of var eid 241742351592924184). The binary has two identities today: target/seon.tree (bin/build's record; bin/check looked for e4763c1e-1ebb3f39.all.edn) and target/seon.program (seon.program/binary-program, which the plan keys its judgements by); they become one digest, and both files' readers ask it. Deletes (owner: find fixes and delete the other implementations): \"current by reach\" (seon.system/stale-binary, seon.system/binary-current's reach), seon.problems/stale-binary's own check, the second identity file, bin/build current, test-runner/base-products and bin/land's own rebuild guess. Evidence of the class [M]: 2026-11-14, bin/land merged a moved main (34ca38f4a9) into data's tip without rebuilding target/seon, so seon.check-test/a-failed-seed-says-failed read a stale binary and went red, a false red that cost a land cycle; the same day a pin change took 3 tries to prove (bin/prove refused it on the standing system, then found its runtime unbuilt). So after any merge, bin/land builds what the merged tree names, once, through runtime-for. Agreed with runtime and the owner, 2026-11-14 (plans/deps-runtime-for.md). What exists: three guesses at the runtime a tree needs (bin/build current, test-runner/base-products, bin/land's bin/build jolt) and the ls-tree read of gitlinks (src/seon/fx.clj:175-214). Change: seon.fx/runtime-for [env] reads the pins from the branch's db (the gitlink entities), compares target/<step>.stamp with them, and answers the built runtime, or builds it, or :seon.process/env-mismatch naming the fields (data's error schema); runtime's start-process! calls it as the one check. Deletes: bin/build current, test-runner/base-products, the ls-tree read. Done: every runner asks runtime-for; a law goes red on one that execs target/seon or target/jolt without it. docs/merging.md#a-branch-lands-with-binland.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-process-on-another-runtime-answers-env-mismatch}, :seon.task/needs #{"seon.source/an-import-writes-each-gitlink-as-an-entity"}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-scripts-environment-is-stated-in-one-place", :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-14 (deps): a shell that had sourced bin/env ran bin/jolt-gates, its JOLT_COMPILER_OPTIONS {:image-fn-forms false} reached seven Jolt gates and turned them red, and its HOME broke a git commit. Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: bin/env still assigns as ${X:-default}, so a caller's value wins (bin/env:8, 9, 13, 16, 21); resources/seon/environment.edn does not exist; seon.fx's child-env-scrub and root-variables (src/seon/fx.clj:137-152) are a second hand list of the same variables (runtime's hand-made-lists audit: a DIAL, spec'd config). Agreed with runtime: one resources/seon/environment.edn, as data, each variable's name, :role (:seon or :jolt), :derived or :caller with the reason, and its schema (data's); config and pins come from the branch. bin/env clears JOLT_ and SEON_ and sets each variable from that file; runtime's once-at-start check (:seon.process/env-mismatch, the :process kind's start) and the child env scrub read the same file. No new script, runner or law (owner, 2026-11-14: \"No more heavy machinery on the claude side\"). Deletes: child-env-scrub's and root-variables' literal lists, derived from the file. Estimate: one EDN read at a start (~1M ticks), cached by the file's digest. Proof, red first: seon.fx-test/a-script-takes-only-the-environment-it-is-given. Plan: plans/deps-script-environment.md. docs/machine.md#processes.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-script-takes-only-the-environment-it-is-given}} {:seon.task/name "a-file-or-git-read-goes-through-the-one-catch-up"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed by deps for runtime (its one catch-up), root's call, 2026-11-14. Root's one-tracker direction, agreed 2026-11-14 (target/root/one-derivation-survey.html): every read a computation makes records through Datahike's di/record-read! as a datom key, so a reaction or a reused verdict is never green on a read nobody recorded (a false green). What exists [M, counts in src]: 31 (slurp ...) calls and 49 git!/lib/sh calls read the outside world directly, past any recorder. Change: the outside is read only by the one catch-up, which turns it into datoms (:file/text, :seon.pin, git facts); every other site reads those datoms, so its reads are recorded; a site that must read outside (a process's output) records what it read as the datom the catch-up would write. Audit each of the 80 sites: inside reads move to the store, edge reads stay in the catch-up. Medium. Proof, red first: a reaction over a file's text reruns when that file changes on disk and the catch-up brings it in."
